Distributing firmware binaries
=============================
The pigeon target statically links arduinoWebSockets, covered by LGPL 2.1 or
later. Shipping only a firmware.bin and this notice is not sufficient. For a
source-accompanied distribution under LGPL 2.1 section 6(a), provide:

* A notice that arduinoWebSockets is used, its copyright notice, and the full
  LGPL text (licenses/WebSockets.txt).
* The complete corresponding library source, including changes and the scripts
  and interface files used to build it. Identify modifications and their dates.
* Pigeon source and/or linkable object files, configuration, build instructions,
  and required build/link materials sufficient for recipients to modify the
  library and relink the firmware. Preserve the exact dependency versions and
  toolchain information. Do not include credentials or device identity data.
* Terms that permit modification for the recipient's own use and reverse
  engineering to debug those modifications.

For downloads, offer those materials alongside the binary (section 6(d)). A
generic link to an upstream repository or the pins in platformio.ini alone is
not a replacement for the corresponding source/build materials. Other LGPL
section 6 options exist; consult the full license for their conditions:
https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html

The ESP32 Arduino framework and toolchain are supplied by espressif32 6.13.0.
Their own component licenses also apply to distributed firmware. The MIT
license for Pigeon source does not replace dependency licenses. Preserve
upstream notices and review their distribution terms when shipping binaries.
In particular, inventory the linked Arduino/ESP-IDF components and preserve
their component notices with each binary distribution. This repository does
not publish prebuilt firmware releases or claim to contain every SDK notice.
